        "problem": "What is the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ in the third-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x) = e^{-2x}?$",
        "worked_solution": "The coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is $\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1).$ Let's compute the necessary derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =4{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =-8{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3!}f^{(3)}(1) =\\dfrac{1}{6} \\left( -8e^{-2} \\right) = -\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}.\n$$\n\nSo the coefficient of $(x-1)^3$ is $-\\dfrac{4}{3e^2}.$",
        "screenshot": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14060752/example-742.png"
      }
